(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608131700")
  (:adr/title "Namespace collisions: one reproduced compile failure, one security property about to be shadowed away, and five files a rename left behind")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-13")
  (:adr/supersedes "")
  (:adr/superseded-by "")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-2608123200" "ADR-2608121000" "ADR-2608124800" "ADR-2608125000" "ADR-2608130800" "ADR-2608071000"))
  (:adr/body
  "## What was run

`nbb scripts/verify-namespace-collisions.cljs --all`, twice on 2026-08-13
(22.8m and 14.1m wall; the spread is other agents on this machine, not the
corpus). 4,045 checked-out repos, 5,438 declared source trees, 16,406 file
paths in 16,374 namespaces.

    collisions: 281 divergent, 1544 identical
    of the divergent, 28 are CO-CLASSPATH
      of those, 19 are ONE repo checked out twice, leaving 9 real collisions
    EXTENSION-SHADOWED: 11 namespaces (1 of them co-classpath)
    self-shadowing: 0 files

## Triage

Every co-classpath collision and every EXTENSION-SHADOWED namespace, 19
distinct namespaces. Class counts: DEFECT 7, DELIBERATE 5, STALE 7,
MIGRATION 0.

| namespace | owners | class |
|---|---|---|
| oidc/core | kotoba-lang/oidc, kotoba-lang/org-openid-oidc | DEFECT |
| kotoba/lang/package_registry | kotoba-core-contracts, kotoba-lang | DEFECT |
| kami/gpu | kotoba-lang/gpu, kami-engine-sdk | DEFECT |
| kami/fsm | kotoba-lang/fsm, kami-engine-sdk | DEFECT |
| kami/backend/browser | kotoba-lang/host (.cljc), kami-engine-sdk (.cljs) | DEFECT |
| manimani/ui | app-chat-shell, manimani-experience-ui, local-manimani | DEFECT, latent |
| murakumo/fleet | kotoba-lang/murakumo, murakumo-langchain | DEFECT, latent |
| pay/core | 7 repos | DELIBERATE |
| pay/facilitator | 4 repos | DELIBERATE |
| pay/x402 | 7 repos | DELIBERATE |
| treasury/core | 8 repos | DELIBERATE |
| itonami/kotoba | cloud-itonami/itonami, network-awai/cloud-itonami | DELIBERATE |
| mangaka/server | cloud-itonami/mangaka | STALE, removed |
| mangaka/store/kotoba | cloud-itonami/mangaka | STALE, removed |
| mangaka/cljs/api | cloud-itonami/mangaka | STALE, removed |
| mangaka/cljs/app | cloud-itonami/mangaka | STALE, removed |
| isekai_gen/config | network-isekai (public copy) | STALE, reported |
| isekai_gen/modal_client | network-isekai (public copy) | STALE, reported |
| rdf_canon/core | kotoba-lang/rdf-canon | STALE, reported |

MIGRATION is 0 and that is a property of the scan, not of the fleet: the
detector's `source-extensions` is `.clj .cljc .cljs`, so a `.kotoba` beside a
`.cljc` is invisible to it. `kotoba-lang/fsm` ships both `kami/fsm.cljc` and
`kami/fsm.kotoba` and the report shows only the `.cljc`. Mid-migration pairs
are `verify-require-graph`'s question, not this one.

## DELIBERATE, and how that was established

**The four payment namespaces are documented vendored copies.** Every non-
upstream file was read: 22 of 22 open with a `VENDORED from ...` header naming
the upstream repo, and 21 name the pinned commit. The upstream files in
`kotoba-lang/pay` and `kotoba-lang/treasury` carry no such header, which is
what makes them the upstreams. Their drift is real and is
`verify-vendored-copies.cljs`'s axis, tracked by ADR-2608121000; it is not a
naming defect and renaming any of them would be wrong.

**`itonami/kotoba` is a JVM/portable split across two repos.**
`cloud-itonami/itonami` ships `.clj` importing `java.net.http` and
`java.util.UUID`; `network-awai/cloud-itonami` ships `.cljc` with those imports
removed and an injected `http-fn`. The `.cljc` docstring states the split and
names the test that covers it. Different root commits, and no closure puts them
together.

## STALE, fixed: cloud-itonami/mangaka

Merged as `51ff4c6aa8fc37276bf51ebf58d153344a0df760`; west pin advanced
`e45999080a4b` to `51ff4c6aa8fc` and verified fast-forward by
`scripts/verify-west-pins.cljs --only mangaka`.

Five files removed. The evidence they were leftovers rather than a platform
split, read rather than inferred:

- `3e0c587` (2026-06-30, `chore: split ai-gftd-project-mangaka into standalone
  repo`) renamed source files by substituting over their CONTENTS as well as
  their names. `server.cljc` differs from `server.clj` in exactly three lines,
  all docstring, all of the form `the Python server.py` becoming `the Python
  server.cljc`. `store/kotoba.cljc` differs in one line, `kotoba_datomic.py`
  becoming `kotoba_datomic.cljc`. `runtime.cljc` is byte-identical to
  `runtime.clj`.
- `cljs/api.cljc` and `cljs/app.cljc` got the same treatment in their `ns`
  forms: both sit at `src/mangaka/cljs/` and declare `mangaka.cljc.api` /
  `mangaka.cljc.app`. Namespace and path disagree, so neither name resolves to
  the file and no tool can load it.
- `f0cac01` (2026-07-19, `fix: recover authoritative mangaka source and
  workflows`) added `server.clj`, `runtime.clj`, `store/kotoba.clj`,
  `cljs/api.cljs`, `cljs/app.cljs` and deleted nothing. 241 files changed, 2
  deletions.
- Neither file carries a reader conditional. The `.clj`/`.cljc` pairs are not a
  platform split, and there is no vendoring header on any of them.

Unreferenced, shown rather than assumed:

- The three same-named `.cljc` are shadowed on the JVM. Confirmed on a two-file
  fixture in this session: a `demo/x.clj` beside a `demo/x.cljc` on one
  `:paths` resolves to the `.clj`. Nothing has loaded them since 2026-07-19.
- The two `mangaka.cljc.*` files are loadable by nobody, per the path/ns
  disagreement above.
- The only ClojureScript build in the repo is `clj/shadow-cljs.edn`, whose
  `:init-fn` was `mangaka.cljc.app/init`. shadow-cljs resolves a namespace to a
  path, and that namespace has no path, so **the Studio build has been
  unbuildable since 2026-06-30** -- `clj/shadow-cljs.edn` has exactly one commit
  in its history, the split -- and `clj/resources/public/js` has never existed.
  Repointed to `mangaka.cljs.app/init`, which is a file that is actually there.
  The `mangaka.cljc.*` mentions in `clj/README.md` and `clj/ARCHITECTURE.md`,
  and the layout lines naming the five removed files, were corrected the same
  way.
- After removal, all 60 source files under `clj/src` and `clj-edge/src` have an
  `ns` matching their path. Before it, two did not.

Deliberately not touched: the same substitution also rewrote the README's
column of PYTHON filenames (`storyboard_db.cljc`, `llm.cljc`, `comfy*.cljc`).
Those name files in another project and restoring them means guessing.

Not verified, stated plainly: the repo's own test suite could not be run. Its
classpath does not resolve on this machine at all -- `jp-go-digital-design-
system` is pinned by both `:git/sha` and `:local/root` and tools.deps refuses
to compare them. That break is pre-existing and independent of this change.

## STALE, reported not removed

Two cases are stale in the same sense but the leftover is a whole sub-project,
not a file, so removing it is an owner call.

**`isekai_gen/config`, `isekai_gen/modal_client`.** The identical corruption
signature appears here: `network-isekai/backend/src/isekai_gen/config.cljc`
says `modal/*.cljc` where `net-isekai-gen/src/isekai_gen/config.clj` says
`modal/*.py`, and that is the only difference between the two files. The
`.cljc` was written by `22f2331` (2026-07-01), whose subject ends `.cljs to
.cljc portability pass`. `net-isekai-gen` was created `722b38f` (2026-07-27,
`restore: net-isekai-gen working tree (history lost)`) and its README states
the split is deliberate: the generation backend holds Modal tokens and B2 keys
and `network-isekai` is public. So the private repo holds the uncorrupted copy
and the public `backend/` tree is the derived one. But `backend/` is eight
files with its own `deps.edn` and `:run` alias, and `isekai-gen.core` requires
`isekai-gen.config` from inside it -- the two flagged files ARE referenced,
within the tree that should probably go as a whole. **Question for the owner:
should `network-isekai/backend/` be deleted now that `net-isekai-gen` exists,
or is the public copy still deployed from?**

**`rdf_canon/core`.** `kotoba-lang/rdf-canon` is a 38-line JVM stub,
`Deterministic RDF quad canonical string and hash helpers`, using
`java.security.MessageDigest`. `kotoba-lang/org-w3-rdf-canon` is a 443-line
`.cljc` implementing RDFC-1.0 with all 86 official vectors passing. Different
root commits, so not one repo twice. Consumers were enumerated over every
`deps.edn` under `orgs/`: `org-w3-rdf-canon` has three
(`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app`, `org-w3-vc-data-integrity`,
`org-w3-json-ld-api`) and **`rdf-canon` has zero**. Both are registered in
`manifest/west.yml`. This is the naming rule working as designed -- the origin
plane name won -- with the subject-plane repo left behind. **Question for the
owner: retire the west entry for `kotoba-lang/rdf-canon`?** No consumer moves.

## DEFECT: `oidc.core`, and it is not theoretical

`kotoba-lang/oidc` and `kotoba-lang/org-openid-oidc` both ship
`src/oidc/core.cljc`, with **zero shared names**:

    kotoba-lang/oidc      verify, verify-once
    org-openid-oidc       base64url-decode-bytes, bytes->utf8-string,
                          base64url-decode, decode-jwt-segments,
                          validate-id-token-claims, parse-discovery-document,
                          userinfo-request

`cloud-itonami/local-itonami` depends on both: on `kotoba-lang/authentication`
(which pins `kotoba-lang/oidc`) and directly on `org-openid-oidc`.
`network-awai/cloud-itonami` does the same, putting
`../../kotoba-lang/authentication/src` straight on its `:paths`.

Measured with `clojure -Spath` in `local-itonami`: `org-openid-oidc/src` is at
position 8, `.gitlibs/.../kotoba-lang/oidc/.../src` at 15. org-openid-oidc
wins. Then reproduced directly:

    $ clojure -M -e \"(require 'authentication.adapters.external-factors)\"
    Syntax error compiling at (authentication/adapters/external_factors.cljc:147:19).
    No such var: oidc/verify-once

That namespace does not compile on this classpath, and has not for as long as
the two have been co-resident. Nothing reported it.

Consumers, enumerated by require form:

- `oidc.core` from `kotoba-lang/oidc` (`verify`, `verify-once`): ONE outside
  its own tests -- `kotoba-lang/authentication/src/authentication/adapters/
  external_factors.cljc:147-148`.
- `oidc.core` from `org-openid-oidc` (`decode-jwt-segments`,
  `validate-id-token-claims`): TWO --
  `network-awai/cloud-itonami/src/cloud_itonami/native/signin.cljc:63` and
  `cloud-itonami/local-itonami/src/local_itonami/signin.cljc:63`.
- `authentication.adapters.external-factors` itself has ONE consumer
  fleet-wide: its own test in `kotoba-lang/authentication`. That bounds the
  blast radius -- the break is real but nothing downstream of it runs.

Cost of a rename: 2 call sites in 1 file if `kotoba-lang/oidc` renames (say to
`oidc.verify`), or 4 call sites in 2 files if `org-openid-oidc` renames. The
repo names are both correct and should not change -- CLAUDE.md's plane rule
explicitly permits one subject across two planes when the READMEs state the
boundary, and `org-openid-oidc` does. **Question for the owner: which of the
two namespaces moves?** The cheaper edit is `kotoba-lang/oidc`, and it is also
the one whose single consumer is already broken.

## DEFECT: `kotoba.lang.package-registry`

Both repos ship `src/kotoba/lang/package_registry.cljc`, and
**`kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang` depends on `kotoba-lang/kotoba-core-contracts`**,
so they are co-classpath by construction.

Ten names are shared -- `registry-version`, `registry-key`, `normalize`,
`record-problems`, `validate`, `lookup`, `resolve-record`, `record->lock-dep`,
`lock-from-requests`, `version-only-request?` -- which is the dangerous shape:
no error at the call site, just different behaviour. The divergence that
matters is in `record->lock-dep`:

- kotoba-lang's emits `:dep/definition-cids`, and its own comment says why:
  `CI2: the alias resolves to the definition identities, so linking by name can
  never silently substitute different code.`
- core-contracts' does NOT emit it. It emits `:dep/ref`, `:dep/build`,
  `:dep/registry-record-cid`, `:dep/provides`, `:dep/consumes`, which
  kotoba-lang's drops.

Neither is a superset. And `kotoba-core-contracts/src/kotoba/lang/
package_contract.cljc` calls `definition-cids-error` on `(:dep/definition-cids
dep)` -- the contract validates a key the registry it ships never produces.

History: `0405a21` (2026-07-27, `Separate contracts and assurance ownership
(#62)`) DELETED all 251 lines of `package_registry.cljc` from kotoba-lang,
moving ownership to core-contracts. `11eac29` (2026-07-28, `feat(package):
registry + conformance fixtures`, empty commit body) added 93 lines of it
straight back. `b6bde5a` (2026-08-03) then grew that copy by 37 lines of the
CI2 work. So the second copy is a re-introduction one day after a deliberate
ownership move, and it is the copy carrying the newer security property.

Who wins depends on which tree you look at, and there are three:

- **On `main` in both checkouts**: `kotoba-lang/kotoba`'s classpath places
  core-contracts at 12 and kotoba-lang at 14. core-contracts wins, and
  `:dep/definition-cids` disappears from every lock.
- **On the pins `kotoba-lang/kotoba` actually resolves today**
  (core-contracts@50de59bf, kotoba-lang@d9fb213c): core-contracts@50de59bf has
  no `src/kotoba/lang/` directory at all -- only `src/kotoba/core` -- so
  kotoba-lang is the sole provider. Its pinned copy is a THIRD content
  (sha 433bbfe3 against 6958d864 on main) and contains zero occurrences of
  `definition-cids`.

So the CI2 property exists only in `kotoba-lang`'s checked-out `main`, is
absent from what runs today, and **advancing `kotoba/deps.edn`'s pins would
silently move the winner to the copy that does not have it**. That is the
decision-relevant fact.

Consumers, enumerated:

- `kotoba-lang/kotoba/src/kotoba/package_admission.clj:30,781` --
  `lock-from-requests`. Depends on BOTH owners.
- `kotoba-lang/security/src/kotoba/security/package_admission.clj:29,772` --
  `lock-from-requests`. Depends on core-contracts only.
- `kotoba-lang/package-registry-ipfs/src/kotoba/package_registry/ipfs.clj:5,44`
  -- `lock-from-requests`. Depends on kotoba-lang only.
- `kotoba-lang/kotoba/test/kotoba/package_registry_test.clj` --
  `version-only-request?`, `lock-from-requests`.
- `kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang/test/kotoba/lang/code_identity_test.clj:18,110` --
  `check-case`, which exists ONLY in kotoba-lang's copy.

`kotoba.lang.package-registry-network` exists only in core-contracts, and
`package_admission.clj` requires it alongside the registry -- so `kotoba` and
`security` cannot simply drop core-contracts.

**Question for the owner: does `kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang` delete its copy and
port `check-case` plus `:dep/definition-cids` into core-contracts, or does
core-contracts' registry move under a different name?** The first is the
smaller edit (one namespace deleted, one function and one `cond->` clause
moved, five consumers unchanged) and it restores the ownership `0405a21`
decided. It is not a pure deletion: `:dep/definition-cids` and `check-case`
must land in core-contracts in the same change or CI2 is lost.

## DEFECT: `kami.gpu`, and the two it is entangled with

Named in ADR-2608123200 as the one of the three `kami.*` collisions that needs
an owner rather than a cleanup, and that holds. Zero shared names, so the loser
fails loudly, but they are different KINDS:

- `kotoba-lang/gpu` -- capability/render-graph data contract: `caps-webgpu`,
  `caps-native`, `caps-console`, `caps-webgl2`, `tiers`, `missing-caps`,
  `pass-runnable?`, `resolve-graph`, `resolve-for`, `caps-from-device`,
  `requires`.
- `kami-engine-sdk` -- runtime protocol: `IGpuBackend` (7 methods), `backend`,
  `ensure-assets!`, `submit!`.

Consumers, by which side's vars they name:

- SDK side (4): `kami-engine-sdk/src/kami/backend/browser.cljs`
  (`IGpuBackend`); `kami-engine-sdk/src/kami/sim.cljc` (`backend`,
  `ensure-assets!`, `submit!`); `kami-engine-sdk/test/kami/runtime_test.cljc`
  (`ensure-assets!`, `IGpuBackend`); **`kotoba-lang/host/src/kami/backend/
  browser.cljc` (`IGpuBackend`)**.
- Extracted side (1): `kotoba-lang/webgpu/test/gpu_test.clj`
  (`caps-from-device`, `caps-webgl2`, `caps-webgpu`, `missing-caps`,
  `pass-runnable?`, `requires`, `resolve-for`).

The `host` entry is why this cannot be resolved by deletion: `host` is the SSoT
of `kami.input`, is a dependency of both `webgpu` and isekai, and needs the
SDK's protocol. `kotoba-lang/webgl` requires `kotoba.gpu` -- a third
implementation that owns its code rather than re-exporting -- and survives
either resolution, which is a pre-paid hedge.

**Question for the owner: which `kami.gpu` renames?** Renaming the extracted
one costs 7 var references in 1 test file plus `kami.gpu.launch`'s docstring;
renaming the SDK's costs `IGpuBackend` in 3 files and the three functions in 2.
The extracted side is the cheaper edit by call-site count; the SDK side is the
one whose name is least descriptive of what it holds.

`kami.backend.browser` is the same decision wearing a different hat and should
be taken with it. `kotoba-lang/host` ships `.cljc` (reader-conditional, `make`
on both platforms, requires `kami.gpu` for `IGpuBackend`); `kami-engine-sdk`
ships `.cljs` (`make`, `BrowserBackend`). ClojureScript prefers `.cljs` over
`.cljc` regardless of classpath order, so the SDK always wins there and host's
`make` is unreachable in any browser build -- and both define `make`, so the
loss is silent. Consumers: `etzhayyim/com-etzhayyim-sip/src/sip/web.cljs:13`,
`kotoba-lang/kami-app-sip/src/sip/web.cljs:13`,
`kami-engine-sdk/examples/hello_scene.cljc:12`,
`kotoba-lang/host/test/host_test.clj:3`. `kami.fsm` is the third and
ADR-2608123200 already costs it out; nothing here changes that analysis.

## DEFECT, latent: `manimani/ui` and `murakumo/fleet`

Reported for completeness. Both are several unrelated implementations under one
name that no closure currently puts together, so neither can bite today; both
become the previous section the first time a dependency is added.

- `manimani.ui`: `kotoba-lang/app-chat-shell/svelte/cljs/src` (565 lines,
  reagent), `kotoba-lang/manimani-experience-ui/src/main` (201 lines, reagent +
  shadow.css), `network-awai/local-manimani/src` (1189 lines `.cljc`, shared
  policy/viewmodel for iOS/Android/web/desktop). The fourth owner,
  `etzhayyim/com-etzhayyim-app-chat-shell`, is `app-chat-shell` at a second
  path -- same root commit, byte-identical.
- `murakumo.fleet`: `kotoba-lang/murakumo` `.clj` (1190 bytes, fleet inventory
  + Tailscale enrichment) versus `kotoba-lang/murakumo-langchain` `.cljc` (1834
  bytes, fleet roster + round-robin ChatModel). `cloud-murakumo-langchain` is
  `murakumo-langchain` at a second path.

## Gate-ability: it cannot be a fleet gate, and here is where it goes instead

**Verdict: `scripts/fleet-ci/gates.edn` is the wrong home and no entry should
be added.**

A fleet gate is shipped only the target repository's own tree. This check is
about two repositories at once -- its whole primitive is `orgs/<org>/<repo>`
across 4,045 checkouts, and `git ls-files orgs/` in the superproject returns 0.
Sent to a node as a `root` gate it would scan an empty `orgs/` directory, find
zero collisions and report PASS. That is worse than absent: it would be a green
check mark asserting an invariant nobody measured.

This workspace has already paid for that mistake once. `root-permit-index` was
removed on 2026-08-13 (ADR-2608130800) after 298 receipts across 259 distinct
tips, every one a failure and not one green, for exactly this reason -- and its
failure message named a broken invariant rather than a missing input. Neither
failure mode is acceptable here.

Nor can it be moved to where its input lives. There is no repository that has
`orgs/` in its tree, because `orgs/` is what west materialises rather than what
any repo contains.

**So it is registered in `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` instead**, the home
ADR-2608125000 built for precisely this class, run by
`scripts/orgs-detector-tick.cljs` on a machine that has `orgs/`. That required
one additive change to the detector: a `--findings` flag emitting
`FINDING<TAB>severity<TAB>key<TAB>detail` and a `SCANNED<TAB>n<TAB>unit` line,
because the tick keys findings by identity so a collision that appeared today
is distinguishable from one that has stood for weeks, and refuses to record a
run as clean without evidence that something was scanned.

`fail` is exactly the set `--check` exits 1 on (the 9 co-classpath divergent
collisions); `warn` is the 10 EXTENSION-SHADOWED namespaces no closure holds
together, which are report-only because they cannot bite until a dependency is
added. Interval 24h and timeout 60m, from the two measured runs.

Proof the flag is additive, since a change that moves counts without an
explanation is a regression until shown otherwise:

- On a 3-repo synthetic fixture, old script and new script with `--all` produce
  **byte-identical** output; with `--findings` the output is byte-identical once
  the `FINDING` and `SCANNED` lines are removed; `--check` exits 1 in all three
  invocations.
- On the real corpus, the collision, suppression, unreachable and identical
  listings are byte-identical between the 03:17 run of the old script and the
  04:14 run of the new one -- 4,343 lines, no diff.
- One line did move: `16406 file paths in 16374 namespaces` became `16408 /
  16376`. Both extra namespaces have a single owner, so they appear nowhere in
  the report and no finding changed. This is concurrent-session churn in the
  shared checkouts, and I could not pin down which two files: the `find` sweep
  over `orgs/` needed to prove it did not finish inside its budget. Stated as
  weaker evidence than the rest.

## What is deliberately out of scope

The 253 divergent namespaces no closure puts on one classpath, and the 1,544
identical ones, were not triaged individually. They are scaffold families --
the actor repos are generated from one template -- and by the detector's own
design they are separated for that reason. The payment ones among them belong
to `verify-vendored-copies.cljs`, which asks the axis that actually finds them.
The 19 suppressed DUPLICATE REGISTRATION namespaces belong to
`verify-duplicate-registrations.cljs`.

Self-shadowing is 0 today. That bucket is worth keeping: it is the one class
this detector answers that no per-repo check can.")))
